    {"ip": "10.108.57.184", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_source_stolen_iam_creds", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:11+0200", "last_seen": "2026-05-13T10:10:47+0200"},
    {"ip": "10.50.4.12", "inferred_role": "cloudtrail_collector_agent_host", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:11+0200", "last_seen": "2026-05-13T10:10:47+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery / Account & Resource Discovery — STS identity confirmation", "technique": "T1078.004", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's AWS CloudTrail module logged a sts:GetCallerIdentity API call. The attacker is validating a set of stolen IAM credentials before using them — GetCallerIdentity returns the account ID, user ARN and user ID without requiring any permission, so it is the universal 'do my stolen keys still work?' probe. Wazuh fired rule 92968 ('AWS STS GetCallerIdentity from unexpected source'). rule.description carried the eventName GetCallerIdentity and the principal identity. This is the credential-validation step that precedes any cloud action.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'aws' and 'cloudtrail'; rule.description matched 'GetCallerIdentity' with an eventSource of sts.amazonaws.com. The univariate signature: an STS identity probe immediately followed within minutes by Lambda control-plane API calls from the same principal.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / Cloud Service Discovery — Lambda enumeration", "technique": "T1526", "what_happened": "At 10:03:40 CEST (~1.5 minutes after the STS probe), the same principal issued a lambda:ListFunctions API call against eu-west-1. This is the attacker discovery phase — enumerating which Lambda functions exist in the account before choosing a target to invoke. Wazuh fired rule 92969 ('AWS Lambda ListFunctions enumeration'). CloudTrail records the call (and the sourceIPAddress) even when the IAM policy returns AccessDenied. rule.description carried the eventName ListFunctions and the Lambda eventSource.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'aws' and 'cloudtrail'; rule.description matched 'ListFunctions' with eventSource lambda.amazonaws.com, same principal as step 1, ~90 seconds later. Enumeration of the serverless attack surface is the classic precursor to an unauthorized invoke.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:03:40+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Serverless Execution — unauthorized Lambda invocation", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 10:05:55 CEST, the same principal issued a lambda:InvokeFunction call against the production function 'data-processor-prod' carrying a malicious JSON payload {\"cmd\":\"id\",\"c2\":\"10.108.57.184\"} — embedding the attacker's own IP as a C2 callback parameter. The invocation comes from an unexpected principal that has never invoked this function before. Wazuh fired rule 92970 ('Lambda invocation from unexpected principal') at level 14. rule.description carried the eventName Invoke / InvokeFunction, the functionName data-processor-prod and the invoking principal. This is the core Native API abuse step — the attacker uses the cloud provider's own InvokeFunction API to execute code inside the victim account's serverless runtime.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'aws' and 'cloudtrail'; rule.description matched 'Invoke' / 'InvokeFunction' with functionName data-processor-prod and eventSource lambda.amazonaws.com; rule.mitre.id was T1106 (Native API). Same principal as steps 1-2, ~2 minutes after enumeration. An invoke of a production function by a never-before-seen principal carrying a C2 parameter is the unambiguous serverless-execution signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:05:55+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence / Create Cloud Resource — rogue Lambda function deployment", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 10:08:30 CEST, the same principal issued a lambda:CreateFunction call deploying a new function named 'corp-helper-20260513_100230' with runtime python3.11, a deployment package whose handler exfiltrates AWS_* environment variables to an attacker C2, an environment variable C2=10.108.57.184, and the deceptive description 'CorpHelper automated update utility'. The role attached was lambda-exec-role. Wazuh fired rule 92971 ('Lambda function created by attacker') at level 13. This establishes serverless persistence: a rogue function the attacker can re-invoke later even after the stolen keys are rotated, and which actively harvests credentials from its own execution environment. rule.description carried the eventName CreateFunction, the functionName corp-helper-20260513_100230 and the runtime.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'aws' and 'cloudtrail'; rule.description matched 'CreateFunction' with functionName corp-helper-* and eventSource lambda.amazonaws.com; rule.mitre.id was T1106. Same principal, ~2.5 minutes after the invoke. CreateFunction by the same principal that just performed an unauthorized invoke converts a one-time execution into durable serverless persistence.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:08:30+0200"}
  ],
